Bessel Functions J and Y
9.1. Definitions and Elementary Properties
Differential Equation
9.1.1   z^{2}\frac{dxw}{dz^{2}}+z\frac{dw}{dz}+(z^{2}-\eta^{2})w=0
Solutions are the Bessel functions of the first kind J_{\nu+}(z), of the second kind Y_{\nu}(z) (also called Weber's function) and of the third kind H_{\nu}^{(1)}(z), H_{\nu}^{(2)}(z) (also called the Hankel functions). Each is a regular (holomorphic) function of z throughout the z-plane cut along the negative real axis, and for fixed z(\neq 0) each is an entire (integral) function of n. When n=\pm n,J_{\nu}(z) has no branch point and is an entire (integral) function of z.
Important features of the various solutions are as follows: J_{\nu}(z)(\partial_{\nu}\geq 0) is bounded as z\to 0 in any bounded range of arg z. J_{\nu}(z) and J_{\nu-}(z) are linearly independent except when r is an integer. J_{\nu}(z) and Y_{\nu}(z) are linearly independent for all values of n.
H_{\nu}^{(1)}(z)\text{ tends to zero as }|z|\to\infty\) in the sector 0<arg z<\pi; H_{\nu}^{(2)}(z) tends to zero as |z|\to\infty\) in the sector -\pi<\arg z<0. For all values of n, H_{\nu}^{(1)}(z) and H_{\nu}^{(2)}(z) are linearly independent.
